2026-11-07T06:33:14.4239189Z 3. Create the system users [34m(uids [30001..30008])[0m and groups [34m(gid 30000)[0m
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4240218Z    that the Nix daemon uses to run builds. To create system users
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4241120Z    in a different range, exit and run this tool again with
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4241825Z    NIX_FIRST_BUILD_UID set.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4242150Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4242524Z 4. Perform the basic installation of the Nix files daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4243006Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4243369Z 5. Configure your shell to import special Nix Profile files, so you
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4244351Z    can use Nix.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4244660Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4244879Z 6. Start the Nix daemon.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4245236Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4245820Z [32m[4;32mWould you like to see a more detailed list of what I will do?
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4251632Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4252093Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4252209Z I will:
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4252453Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4252706Z  - make sure your computer doesn't already have Nix files
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4253182Z    (if it does, I will tell you how to clean them up.)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4253633Z  - create local users (see the list above for the users I'll make)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4254351Z  - create a local group (nixbld)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4254704Z  - install Nix in /nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4255023Z  - create a configuration file in /etc/nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4255460Z  - set up the "default profile" by creating some Nix-related files in
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4256044Z    /root
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4267611Z  - back up /etc/bash.bashrc to /etc/bash.bashrc.backup-before-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4268477Z  - update /etc/bash.bashrc to include some Nix configuration
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4280176Z  - load and start a service (at /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.service
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4280896Z    and /etc/systemd/system/nix-daemon.socket) for nix-daemon
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4281404Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4282584Z [32m[4;32mReady to continue?
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4283291Z [0mNo TTY, assuming you would say yes :)
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4303239Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4303782Z [34m---- let's talk about sudo -----------------------------------------------------
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4315129Z [0mThis script is going to call sudo a lot. Normally, it would show you
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4316095Z exactly what commands it is running and why. However, the script is
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4316839Z run in a headless fashion, like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4317291Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4317816Z   $ curl -L https://nixos.org/nix/install | sh
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4318471Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4318973Z or maybe in a CI pipeline. Because of that, I'm going to skip the
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4320081Z verbose output in the interest of brevity.
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4320475Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4320675Z If you would like to
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4321143Z see the output, try like this:
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4321456Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4321793Z   $ curl -L -o install-nix https://nixos.org/nix/install
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4322359Z   $ sh ./install-nix
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4322517Z 
2026-11-07T06:33:14.4322559Z 
